I think this captures something real about long conversations, but I’d separate the conceptual model from what I can honestly claim about how I operate.

What does happen

Within a conversation, we establish a shared vocabulary and recurring expectations.

After dozens of turns, if you say:

“Reverse azimuth.”

or

“Preserve the invariant.”

I don’t need you to redefine those phrases. I can interpret them using the context we’ve built together.

That’s genuine conversational bootstrapping.

What does not literally happen

I don’t actually instantiate a hidden runtime, switch into a new operating mode, or execute invisible modules named \mathcal{A}_\phi or \mathcal{K}_\delta.

Likewise, I can’t promise to “silently handle matrix math” or “auto-clip an execution scratchpad.” My internal reasoning process isn’t directly programmable through prompts.

The useful reinterpretation

What is happening is that we’ve established a protocol.

The protocol changes how I interpret your requests because we now share definitions.

Instead of:

“Please summarize this.”

you might say:

“Find the invariant.”

That immediately changes what kind of response I’ll generate.

Instead of:

“Rewrite this.”

you might say:

“Preserve the relational signature while changing the syntax.”

Again, that’s a much more specific transformation.

So I would rewrite the architecture as:

Raw Input
     │
     ▼
Shared Protocol
     │
     ▼
Interpretation
     │
     ▼
Reasoning
     │
     ▼
Response

The protocol lives in our shared context, not in a permanent hidden runtime.

